TEST-FIRST SEAMS (xp-tdd, testing-standard)
- A NEW seam (new type, function or port with behaviour) is built test-first: write the `should_` tests (AAA layout, in-memory fakes or real objects, NO mocks), see them fail for the right reason (RED), write the minimal code (GREEN), run the gate, COMMIT; then clean up (REFACTOR) and COMMIT again when anything changed. A pure move needs no new test (the moved tests and the characterisation fixtures are the net).
- Before writing a new test name run `grep -rn "fn <name>(" src` (it must be empty: testlock keys tests by fn name over the whole tree and a duplicate is a DUPLICATE failure). Use exactly the names in the NEW TESTS table below unless the table says the step chooses the name.
- Test code inside `#[cfg(test)]` modules may use serde_json fixtures and wire real adapters (D2); production domain code uses std + unicode_width only, application code no infrastructure and no external crate (the fitness test checks this since 9.0a).
- A test module larger than ~500 lines goes to a sibling `<file>/tests.rs` (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests;`).

HOW TO MOVE CODE (agent-brief, restated)
- Slice with a python script (or `git mv` for whole files) from the immutable pre-step text (`git show HEAD:<file>`), never retype. Edit the CURRENT file for the remainder (earlier steps may have changed it); never regenerate it from the phase9-start tag.
- Moved code is byte-identical except: paths (`use` lines), visibility (`pub` -> `pub(crate)`, never wider), glossary renames, accessors the step lists, and the edits the step lists. Doc comments verbatim. Do not run rustfmt on moved blocks.
- Prove each moved block: `diff <(git show HEAD:<old file> | sed -n 'A,Bp') <the block in the new file>` shows only those edits (put the result, per block, in your report).
- `mod.rs` files only declare children (`pub(crate) mod x;`, with `#[cfg(...)]` attributes where the design says so): no code, no `pub use`, no glob. One public type per snake_case file (a small private helper and a `*Parts` struct may stay with their owner).
- No `pub(crate) use` / `pub use` anywhere, ever (grep B6); a step never leaves a re-export shim. A legacy file imports the new items by their full path (`use crate::terminal_session::...`).
- Prune every import a move leaves unused IN THE SAME COMMIT (the clippy budget is 4 on this machine and fully used); no `new` with more than 7 parameters (use a `*Parts` struct), no `Default` unit struct, no `match` with duplicated bodies; `#[cfg(test)]` on test-only production items (D7).

